What a p-value Is NOT
A p-value is not:

the probability that the null hypothesis is true;
the probability that the alternative hypothesis is true;
the probability that the result occurred “by chance”.

These interpretations are incorrect.

Why Confidence Intervals Are Useful
Confidence intervals provide information about:

direction of the effect;
magnitude of the effect;
precision of the estimate.

They are often more informative than p-values alone.

When Should Non-Parametric Tests Be Considered?
Non-parametric tests may be useful when:

distributions are highly skewed;
substantial outliers are present;
sample sizes are very small;
assumptions are clearly violated.

However, they are not automatically superior and should not be used simply because a normality test is statistically significant.

Assumptions
The independent t-test assumes:

observations are independent;
the outcome variable is numerical;
the distributions are approximately normal;
variability is reasonably similar between groups.

Mean versus Median
Consider a distribution containing a few unusually large values.
The mean is pulled towards those extreme observations, whereas the median remains relatively stable.

Interactive Demonstration: Mean versus Median
Move the final observation and observe how the mean and median respond.

viewof extreme_value = Inputs.range(
  [20, 120],
  {
    value: 80,
    step: 1,
    label: "Final observation"
  }
)







data_values = [45, 50, 55, 60, extreme_value]

mean_value = d3.mean(data_values)

median_value = d3.median(data_values)

Plot.plot({
  width: 700,
  height: 180,
  marginLeft: 50,
  x: {
    domain: [20, 120],
    label: "Observed value"
  },
  y: {
    domain: [0, 1],
    axis: null
  },
  marks: [
    Plot.ruleY([0.5], {stroke: "#cccccc"}),
    Plot.dot(data_values, {
      x: d =&gt; d,
      y: 0.5,
      r: 8,
      fill: "#3A7CA5"
    }),
    Plot.ruleX([mean_value], {
      stroke: "#C73E3A",
      strokeWidth: 3
    }),
    Plot.ruleX([median_value], {
      stroke: "#002147",
      strokeWidth: 3,
      strokeDasharray: "5,5"
    }),
    Plot.text(
      [`Mean = ${mean_value.toFixed(1)}`],
      {
        x: mean_value,
        y: 0.82,
        fill: "#C73E3A",
        fontSize: 14
      }
    ),
    Plot.text(
      [`Median = ${median_value.toFixed(1)}`],
      {
        x: median_value,
        y: 0.18,
        fill: "#002147",
        fontSize: 14
      }
    )
  ]
})



































TipWhat to notice



As the final observation becomes more extreme, the mean moves more strongly than the median. This is why the median is often preferred for skewed clinical measurements.








ImportantKey Idea



The median is generally more robust to extreme values than the mean.

